{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791668239,"nonce":"x55tLA0g3lB1ly+PolIOrtJjlVk=","title":"A styled font lookup settles for a wrong weight or style among the preloaded faces","message":"`FontRegistry` preloads `KNOWN_FONT_PATHS` and only scans the system when a lookup finds nothing. fontdb's query returns the nearest face among what is loaded, so a weight or style the preload lacks resolves to whatever is there: on Windows every weight was the regular face (d36d399), and on macOS system italic resolved to upright SF while the italic face sat unscanned. Preload lists can only name the files someone thought of.\n\nFix: after a styled lookup, if the face is not the weight and style asked for and is not a variable face (a `wght` axis takes any weight), scan the system once and ask the face's own family again, keeping the face when the family has nothing closer. Staying in the family keeps a newly scanned family from taking over a weight.\n\nCost: a full scan maps each font file only to read its names, weight and style, then keeps path plus metadata; face data is mapped when text first uses the face. On a dev Mac, 852 faces in 400 files (834 MB on disk) added about 0.5-0.85 MB resident and took about 140 ms warm, 400 ms cold. The scan runs on the thread doing the lookup, once per registry; it already ran on any lookup miss.\n\nEffect on macOS, checked per lookup on a fresh registry: the system face (variable SF) never scans at any weight; sans and mono bold resolve from the preload; system italic now scans and gets SF Italic instead of upright SF; serif bold scanned before and lands on the same Times New Roman Bold.\n\nTest: `registry::tests::a_weight_the_preload_lacks_is_found_by_scanning_once` builds a registry that preloaded regular Arial and whose scan supplies a bold one (the bundled Arial with its OS/2 weight rewritten); it fails if the lookup keeps the preloaded face.","files":null}]}